WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.210
My pupils trained a small model to sort

2
00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:06.430
drawings into circles, crosses and lines.

3
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.620
It was sure: a circle.

4
00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.170
Then, in the lab, we changed eight pixels

5
00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.870
in just the right way, and it said cross,

6
00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.430
though the picture looked the same to us.

7
00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:20.970
Today: pictures that fool a

8
00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.610
model, why they work, and how to

9
00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:24.910
test a model against them.

10
00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.500
An adversarial example is an input

11
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.740
changed on purpose, just a little, so

12
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.180
that a model gets it wrong.

13
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.130
People see no difference.

14
00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.610
The model reads tiny values we barely

15
00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:41.470
notice, and the attacker pushes exactly

16
00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:41.990
those.

17
00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:44.820
It's not only pictures.

18
00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:48.070
In tests, stickers on a road

19
00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:49.830
sign fooled sign readers.

20
00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:52.730
A few changed letters can slip

21
00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:53.950
a message past a filter.

22
00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:57.490
And sounds we can hardly hear can

23
00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:58.970
steer a voice assistant.

24
00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.870
Think of a pupil who learned to spot a

25
00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.070
cow by the green grass around it.

26
00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:06.830
Show them a cow on a

27
00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.250
beach, and they're lost.

28
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.880
Models learn shortcuts like that

29
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.440
too, and attackers aim at them.

30
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.980
One defence is adversarial training: make

31
00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:20.800
tricky examples on purpose, label them

32
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.700
correctly, and train on them too.

33
00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.230
The model gets harder to fool,

34
00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:26.890
but never impossible.

35
00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:29.950
You can test any model's

36
00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:31.130
answer in a similar way.

37
00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:34.210
Nudge the input a little, both ways,

38
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.110
and see if the label changes.

39
00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:39.070
If it flips, that answer is

40
00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.250
fragile: don't let it decide alone.

41
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:43.020
Quick check!

42
00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:44.950
A model says it's

43
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:46.670
ninety-nine percent sure.

44
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.940
Does that mean an attacker can't fool it?

45
00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:51.360
No.

46
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.790
A model can be sure and wrong at the

47
00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:57.770
same time: in the lab, a confident

48
00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.130
answer flips with six to eight pixels.

49
00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.920
Confidence is a number the model

50
00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.160
gives itself, not a guarantee.

51
00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:09.560
So where a decision matters, like a

52
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.620
pupil's mark, a health photo or a gate

53
00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.500
that opens, don't let one model decide.

54
00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:18.810
Combine signals, test with small

55
00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.190
changes, and keep a person in charge.

56
00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:24.870
In the app, train the shapes model,

57
00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:27.730
then raise the pixels allowed until

58
00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.030
the right picture flips.

59
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.160
Press “Train it tough” and attack

60
00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.580
again: fewer attacks get through.

61
00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:36.540
Your turn.

62
00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.920
A school plans to take attendance with a

63
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.040
face-recognition camera, and nothing else.

64
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.080
What's the safest plan?

65
00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.800
Use it as a helper, with a person

66
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.060
checking and another way in.

67
00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.490
Remember: tiny chosen changes

68
00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:56.450
can fool a sure model.

69
00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.680
Training it tough helps,

70
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.160
but isn't perfect.

71
00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.920
Test with small nudges, and

72
00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:03.880
keep a person deciding.

73
00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:07.630
Try it in class tomorrow: one

74
00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:09.790
pupil is the “model” and sorts

75
00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:11.730
drawings by a secret rule.

76
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.280
The class hunts for the smallest

77
00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.380
change that flips its answer.

78
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.720
Then ask: how could we make

79
00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.320
the rule harder to fool?
